<chapter>
	<title>Leveling Up PreTeXt</title>

	<introduction>
		<p>
			<em>Everything you need during today's session. Keep this page open in a tab.</em>
		</p>
    <objectives>
      <introduction><p>At the end of this tutorial you will have...</p></introduction>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Created a free GitHub account.</p></li>
        <li><p>Created a GitHub Repository and Codespace for authoring <pretext/> in your web browser.</p></li>
        <li><p>Learned the first steps to editing a <pretext/> document.</p></li>
        <li><p>Converted your document to both <latex/>-generated PDF and accessible <init>HTML</init>.</p></li>
        <li><p>Deployed your <init>HTML</init> to the web via GitHub Pages.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </objectives>
		<assemblage>
			<title>Before You Start</title>
			<p>
				Make sure you are signed in to <c>github.com</c> in your browser.
				Then open the workshop template repository at:
			</p>
			<p>
				<url href="https://github.com/PreTeXtBook/pretext-codespace">
					<c>github.com/PreTeXtBook/pretext-codespace</c>
				</url>
			</p>
		</assemblage>
	</introduction>

	<!-- ================================================= -->
	<!-- Section: From Compuational Seminar Talk           -->
	<!-- ================================================= -->
	<section xml:id="getting-started-w-pretext">
		<title><pretext /> Setup Guide</title>

		<subsection xml:id="workspace-setup">
			<title>Workspace Setup</title>
			<p>
				The quickest and easiest way to get started with <pretext /> is through GitHub.
				Using a GitHub Codespace means you don't need to install anything locally —
				everything runs in your browser with all dependencies preconfigured.
				Here's how to set it up:
			</p>

			<p>
				<dl>
					<li>
						<title>Create a GitHub Account</title>
						<p>
							<ol>
								<li>Go to <url href="https://github.com">GitHub</url>.</li>
								<li>Create an account (it's free for individual users).</li>
							</ol>
						</p>
					</li>

					<li>
						<title>Import the <pretext /> Codespace Template</title>
						<p>
							<ol>
								<li>Visit the official <url href="https://github.com/PreTeXtBook/pretext-codespace"><pretext /> Codespace</url> repository on GitHub.</li>
								<li>
									<p>
										<sidebyside widths="12% 38% 2% 24% 24%">
											<p>Click</p>
											<p><image width="100%" source="use-this-template.png"/></p>
											<p/>
											<p>and choose</p>
											<p/>
										</sidebyside>
										<q>Create a New Repository</q>.
									</p>
								</li>
								<li>
									<p>
										In the new window, fill in:
										<ul>
											<li><c>Repository name</c></li>
											<li><c>Description</c> — optional.</li>
											<li><c>Visibility</c> — choose <q>Public</q> or <q>Private</q>.</li>
										</ul>
										Then click <q>Create repository</q>.
										GitHub will copy the official <pretext /> codespace into your account as a new repo.
									</p>
								</li>
							</ol>
						</p>
					</li>

					<li>
						<title>Start Your Codespace</title>
						<p>
							<ol>
								<li>If you weren't automatically redirected, go to your GitHub account and open the new repository you just created.</li>
								<li>
									<p>
										<sidebyside widths="12% 18% 2% 68% 0%">
											<p>Click</p>
											<p><image width="100%" source="code-button.png"/></p>
											<p/>
											<p>and open the <q>Codespaces</q> tab.</p>
											<p/>
										</sidebyside>
									</p>
								</li>
								<li>
									<p>
										<sidebyside widths="35% 48% 17%">
											<p>In this tab, click</p>
											<p><image width="100%" source="create-codespace.png"/></p>
											<p/>
										</sidebyside>
									</p>
								</li>
								<li>
									The Codespace will begin setting up. This may take a few minutes the first time.
									When complete, you'll have a fully functional <pretext /> workspace running in your browser.
								</li>
							</ol>
						</p>
					</li>
				</dl>
			</p>
		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="minimal-project">
			<title>A Minimal Project</title>

			<introduction>
				<p>
					Once your Codespace loads, it'll will look like so: 
				</p>

				<image source="fresh-codespace.png"/>

				<p>
					It's ready for <pretext/>, but it needs a few files first. If you click on the <c>README.md</c> and scroll down to <q>Creating a New Project</q>, you'll find some directions for initiating a <pretext/> project.
				</p>
			</introduction>

			<subsubsection xml:id="creating-a-new-book">
				<title>Creating a New Book</title>
					
				<p>
					<dl>
						<li>
							<title>Creating a New <pretext/> Project</title>
							<p>
								<ol>
									<li>Hit <c>F1</c> to open the command palette.</li>
									<li>Type <q>pretext</q>.</li>
									<li>Scroll down and select <q><pretext/>: New Project</q>.</li>
									<li>Select the type of document you want to create. For this demo, we'll choose <c>book</c>.</li>
									<li>Select the location of your project, but the default should be the one you want.</li>
								</ol>
							</p>
						</li>
					</dl>
				</p>

				<p>
					Completing these steps will populate your codespace with all the files you need to author in <pretext />. In fact, it also provides a template that build right-away.
				</p>

				<image source="new-book.png"/>
			</subsubsection>

			<subsubsection xml:id="your-first-build">
				<title>Your First Build</title>
				<p>
					<dl>
						<li>
							<title>Building A <pretext/> Book</title>
							<p>
								<ol>
									<li>
										<p>
											Locate the <c>▷ PreTeXt</c> button on the bottom tool bar.
											<sidebyside>
												<p><image width="100%" source="pretext-button.png"/></p>
											</sidebyside>
										</p>
									</li>
									<li>To build the book, select <q>Build default target</q>.</li>
									<li>The default is the <c>html</c> version of the book. Let's also build a <c>pdf</c> version of the book as well.</li>
									<li>Click on the <c>▷ PreTeXt</c> button and select <q>Build Another Target</q> followed by the <q>print</q> option.</li>
									<li>The results of both of these builds should now be in the <c>output</c> folder.</li>
								</ol>
							</p>
						</li>
					</dl>
				</p>
			</subsubsection>

			<subsubsection xml:id="viewing-output">
				<title>Viewing the Output</title>
				<p>
					<dl>
						<li>
							<title>Viewing A <pretext/> Book</title>
							<p>
								<ol>
									<li>Now, we can view both the <c>html</c> and <c>pdf</c> version of the template book.</li>
									<li>To view them, click on the <c>▷ PreTeXt</c> button again and select <q>View Full Document</q> then choose either the <q>web</q> or <q>print</q> option.</li>
									<li>In either case, a new broswer window should open. If the <c>html</c> doesn't display in the broswer, try refreshing the page.</li>
								</ol>
							</p>
						</li>
					</dl>
				</p>
			</subsubsection>
		</subsection>
	
		<subsection xml:id="having-a-look-around">
			<title>Having A Look Around</title>

			<p>
				Once your Codespace loads, you'll see a ready-to-use <pretext /> project folder.
				You could begin writing immediately, but before doing so it's helpful to understand
				the structure and purpose of the key folders and files.
			</p>

			<assemblage>
				<title>Notable Folders &amp; Files</title>
				<p>
					<dl>
						<li>
							<title><c>source</c></title>
							<p>
								This folder contains your <pretext /> document files. Typically separated by chapter or section, plus a <c>main.ptx</c> file, which pulls them together.
							</p>
						</li>

						<li>
							<title><c>output</c></title>
							<p>
								The <c>output</c> folder is automatically generated when you build your project and contains the final product, ready to view or publish.
								You don't edit anything here.
							</p>
						</li>

						<li>
							<title><c>assets</c></title>
							<p>
								A general-purpose folder for supporting materials like images, data files, videos, etc. When you reference these in your source, <pretext /> includes them automatically.
							</p>
						</li>
					</dl>
				</p>
			
				<p>
					<dl>
						<li>
							<title><c>project.ptx</c></title>
							<p>
								The central configuration file that describes your entire project. This is where you specify the formats you want to generate and what files are needed to build them.
							</p>
						</li>

						<li>
							<title><c>main.ptx</c></title>
							<p>
								The entry point of your <pretext/> document that ties together all chapters or sections. It's like the <q>table of contents</q> of your project.
							</p>
						</li>

						<li>
							<title><c>publication.ptx</c></title>
							<p>
								This settings file that contains all the visual knobs for customizing the output of your documents.
							</p>
						</li>
					</dl>
				</p>
			</assemblage>
		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="first-build">
			<title>Your First Build</title>

			<p>
				Now that you've explored the workspace, it's time to build your first <pretext /> document.
				The template repository already includes a complete working example, so you can test the build process immediately.
			</p>

			<assemblage>
				<title>Building the Web Version</title>
				<p>
					<ol>
						<li>In your Codespace, open the integrated terminal (at the bottom of the editor).</li>
						<li>
							Type the following command and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>:
							<codeblock>pretext build web</codeblock>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								The build process will begin. You'll see messages as <pretext /> processes your source files, applies templates,
								and generates HTML in the <c>output/web</c> folder.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							When the build completes, you'll see a message indicating success.
							The generated files can be previewed directly in the browser.
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>

				<p>
					This command produces a full-featured, navigable website with automatic numbering, cross-references, and MathJax-rendered equations.
				</p>
			</assemblage>

			<assemblage>
				<title>Previewing Your Output</title>
				<p>
					<ol>
						<li>In the Codespace file explorer, expand the <c>output</c> folder.</li>
						<li>Open the <c>web</c> subfolder, then right-click on <c>index.html</c>.</li>
						<li>Choose <q>Preview</q> → <q>Preview in Editor</q> to view it directly inside your Codespace tab, or select <q>Open in Browser</q> for a full window view.</li>
					</ol>
				</p>

				<p>
					You should now see a professional, interactive web page version of your document complete with a table of contents, equations, and styled sections.
				</p>
			</assemblage>

			<assemblage>
				<title>Building a PDF (Optional)</title>
				<p>
					<ol>
						<li>To generate a PDF version, use the following command:</li>
						<codeblock>pretext build pdf</codeblock>
						<li>
							<p>
								This will run a LaTeX-based build to produce a typeset PDF.
								The result will appear in <c>output/pdf/</c>.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>

				<p>
					The PDF build may take a bit longer, especially the first time it downloads LaTeX packages.
				</p>
			</assemblage>

			<assemblage>
				<title>What's Happening Behind the Scenes</title>
				<p>
					When you run a build, <pretext /> uses your <c>publication.ptx</c> file to decide:
					<ul>
						<li>Which outputs to generate (HTML, PDF, EPUB, etc.)</li>
						<li>How to apply templates, styling, and metadata</li>
						<li>Where to place the results (usually in <c>output/&lt;format&gt;/</c>)</li>
					</ul>
				</p>

				<p>
					Because everything is defined structurally rather than visually, your math, examples, and exercises automatically appear with consistent numbering and styling across all outputs.
				</p>
			</assemblage>

			<assemblage>
				<title>Quick Recap</title>
				<ul>
					<li><code>pretext build web</code> — generates a navigable HTML version of your document.</li>
					<li><code>pretext build pdf</code> — creates a typeset print version (optional).</li>
					<li>Outputs appear inside the <c>output/</c> folder — no manual file management required.</li>
					<li>All formatting, numbering, and linking are handled automatically by <pretext />.</li>
				</ul>

				<p>
					You now have a working build process and a functional document. 
					In the next section, we'll explore how to edit content and customize your project.
				</p>
			</assemblage>
		</subsection>

	</section>

	<!-- ================================================= -->
	<!-- Section: Launch Codespace                   -->
	<!-- ================================================= -->
	<section xml:id="launch-codespace">
		<title>1. Launch Your Codespace</title>

		<p>
			<em>Important: we start your Codespace now and then leave it alone for about half an hour.
			First-time setup takes 5–10 minutes, so while it boots in the background we will practice
			PreTeXt in a sandbox. Do not close the Codespace tab.</em>
		</p>

		<ol>
			<li>
				<p>
					On the <c>pretext-codespace</c> page, click the green
					<term>Use this template</term> button (top right), then choose
					<term>Create a new repository</term>.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Give your repository a name (for example, <c>my-pretext-workshop</c>),
					leave it set to <term>Public</term>, and click
					<term>Create repository from template</term>.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					On your new repository page, click the green <c>&lt; &gt; Code</c> button,
					switch to the <term>Codespaces</term> tab, and click
					<term>Create codespace on main</term>.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					A new tab opens and begins building your workspace.
					<em>Leave this tab open. Do not touch it.</em>
					It will finish on its own while you work in the sandbox.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Open a <em>new browser tab</em> and go to
					<url href="https://pretext.plus/tryit"><c>pretext.plus/tryit</c></url>.
					Keep both tabs open for the rest of the workshop.
				</p>
			</li>
		</ol>

		<assemblage>
			<title>✓ You know it worked when…</title>
			<p>
				<ul>
					<li>Your Codespace tab is visibly loading or says <q>Setting up your codespace.</q></li>
					<li>You can see the <c>pretext.plus/tryit</c> editor in a different tab.</li>
					<li>You are not tempted to click around in the Codespace tab.</li>
				</ul>
			</p>
		</assemblage>

		<p>
			<em>Once your Codespace is ready, you should see a file explorer on the left and a blue bar at the bottom of the window.</em>
			<ol>
				<li>
					<p>
						Press <kbd>F1</kbd> (or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd>) to
						open the command palette. Type <c>pretext new</c> and select
						<term>PreTeXt: New Project</term>.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						When asked what kind of project, choose <term>book</term>. When asked where
						to put it, press <kbd>Enter</kbd> to accept the default location.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						The window reloads with a new project. Look for the <c>source</c> folder
						on the left and click to expand it.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						Find and open <c>main.ptx</c> (or the first chapter file in <c>source/</c>).
						This is where your lesson will live.
					</p>
				</li>
			</ol>
		</p>

	</section>

	<!-- ================================================= -->
	<!-- Section: Starter document                           -->
	<!-- ================================================= -->
	<section xml:id="starter-document">
		<title>2. Your Starter Document</title>

		<p>
			Inside <c>main.ptx</c>, find the first <c>&lt;section&gt;</c> inside the first
			<c>&lt;chapter&gt;</c>. Replace everything between the opening and closing
			<c>&lt;section&gt;</c> tags with the block below. Save the file
			(<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd>).
		</p>

		<program language="xml">
			<code>
<![CDATA[<title>Right Triangles</title>

<p>This short lesson introduces right triangles and
the Pythagorean theorem.</p>

<definition xml:id="def-right-triangle">
<statement>
	<p>A <term>right triangle</term> is a triangle
	with one angle measuring exactly 90 degrees.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>The Pythagorean theorem states that
<m>a^2 + b^2 = c^2</m>, where <m>c</m> is the
length of the hypotenuse.</p>

<example>
<title>A 3-4-5 triangle</title>
<statement>
	<p>Show that a triangle with sides 3, 4, and 5
	is a right triangle.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
	<p>Check: <m>3^2 + 4^2 = 9 + 16 = 25 = 5^2</m>.</p>
</solution>
</example>

<exercise>
<statement>
	<p>Find the length of the hypotenuse of a right
	triangle with legs of length 5 and 12.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<p>For a reminder of the terminology,
see <xref ref="def-right-triangle"/>.</p>]]>
			</code>
		</program>

		<assemblage>
			<title>Now make it yours</title>
			<p>
				Change the chapter title. Rename the section. Swap the topic for something you
				actually teach — replace the definition with your own, rewrite the example,
				change the exercise. The structure stays the same. The content is yours.
			</p>
		</assemblage>
	</section>

	<!-- ================================================= -->
	<!-- Section: Build, View, Deploy                      -->
	<!-- ================================================= -->
	<section xml:id="build-view-deploy">
		<title>3. Build, View, Deploy</title>

		<p>
			Find the <term>▷ PreTeXt</term> button in the blue bar at the very bottom of the
			Codespace window. That button is your control panel.
		</p>

		<paragraphs>
			<title>Build — turn your source into a webpage</title>
			<p>
				Click <term>▷ PreTeXt</term> → <term>Build default target</term>. Wait for the
				terminal to finish. <em>You must Build again every time you change your source.</em>
			</p>
		</paragraphs>

		<paragraphs>
			<title>View — preview your webpage</title>
			<p>
				Click <term>▷ PreTeXt</term> → <term>View</term>. A preview tab opens. After every
				edit, Build again and refresh the preview.
			</p>
		</paragraphs>

		<paragraphs>
			<title>Deploy — publish to a public URL</title>
			<p>
				Click <term>▷ PreTeXt</term> → <term>Deploy</term>. Watch the terminal for your
				<c>github.io</c> link. It may take a few minutes after the command finishes before
				the site is actually live.
			</p>
		</paragraphs>

		<assemblage>
			<title>✓ You're done when…</title>
			<p>
				<ul>
					<li>You have a <c>github.io</c> URL in the terminal.</li>
					<li>You can open that URL in a new tab and see your lesson.</li>
					<li>You are ready to share the link with a colleague.</li>
				</ul>
			</p>
		</assemblage>
	</section>

	<!-- ================================================= -->
	<!-- Section: Troubleshooting                          -->
	<!-- ================================================= -->
	<section xml:id="troubleshooting">
		<title>4. If You Get Stuck</title>

		<assemblage>
			<title>Build failed with a red error</title>
			<p>
				Almost always a missing or mismatched tag. Look at the error message for a line
				number, and check that every <c>&lt;tag&gt;</c> has a matching <c>&lt;/tag&gt;</c>.
				When in doubt, paste the starter block again and edit more carefully.
			</p>
		</assemblage>

		<assemblage>
			<title>Preview tab is blank, stale, or won't load</title>
			<p>
				Build again first, then refresh the preview tab. The preview does not update
				automatically. If that does not help, close the preview tab and run
				<term>▷ PreTeXt</term> → <term>View</term> again.
			</p>
		</assemblage>

		<assemblage>
			<title>Codespace closed, timed out, or went to sleep</title>
			<p>
				Go back to your repository on <c>github.com</c>, click the <c>&lt; &gt; Code</c>
				button, open the <term>Codespaces</term> tab, and click your existing codespace
				to resume it. Your files are still there.
			</p>
		</assemblage>

		<assemblage>
			<title>Something else is wrong</title>
			<p>
				Raise your hand. We will come to you. You are almost certainly not the only one.
			</p>
		</assemblage>
	</section>

	<!-- ================================================= -->
	<!-- Section: LaTeX bridge                             -->
	<!-- ================================================= -->
	<section xml:id="latex-bridge">
		<title>5. LaTeX → PreTeXt</title>

		<p>
			<em>If you already write LaTeX, most of what you know transfers directly. The math
			inside the tags is still LaTeX.</em>
		</p>

		<table>
			<title>Common LaTeX patterns and their PreTeXt equivalents</title>
			<tabular top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" halign="left">
				<col right="minor"/>
				<col right="minor"/>
				<row header="yes" bottom="minor">
					<cell><alert>LaTeX</alert></cell>
					<cell><alert>PreTeXt</alert></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><c>\(x^2\)</c></cell>
					<cell><c>&lt;m&gt;x^2&lt;/m&gt;</c></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><c>\[ \int f \]</c></cell>
					<cell><c>&lt;me&gt;\int f&lt;/me&gt;</c></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><c>\section{Title}</c></cell>
					<cell><c>&lt;section&gt;&lt;title&gt;Title&lt;/title&gt;</c></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><c>\begin{itemize}</c></cell>
					<cell><c>&lt;ul&gt;</c></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><c>\item text</c></cell>
					<cell><c>&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;text&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;</c></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><c>\begin{definition}</c></cell>
					<cell><c>&lt;definition&gt;&lt;statement&gt;&lt;p&gt;</c></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><c>\emph{word}</c></cell>
					<cell><c>&lt;em&gt;word&lt;/em&gt;</c></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><c>\textbf{word}</c></cell>
					<cell><c>&lt;term&gt;word&lt;/term&gt;</c></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><c>\ref{label}</c></cell>
					<cell><c>&lt;xref ref="label"/&gt;</c></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>
		</table>
	</section>

	<!-- ================================================= -->
	<!-- Section: After the workshop                       -->
	<!-- ================================================= -->
	<section xml:id="after-workshop">
		<title>6. After the Workshop</title>

		<p>
			<em>Three ways to keep going, depending on how you like to work.</em>
		</p>

		<paragraphs>
			<title>Keep using Codespaces (recommended)</title>
			<p>
				Your repository is yours. Come back anytime, open the Codespace from the
				<c>&lt; &gt; Code</c> button, and continue editing. Your lesson is already
				deployed, so re-running <term>Deploy</term> updates the live site.
			</p>
		</paragraphs>

		<paragraphs>
			<title>Prefer not to use GitHub at all?</title>
			<p>
				PreTeXt.Plus offers a free tier at
				<url href="https://pretext.plus"><c>pretext.plus</c></url> that lets you
				create up to 10 projects with shareable links — no GitHub account required.
				It is a great fit for quick drafts and one-off documents. The same PreTeXt
				markup you learned today works there too.
			</p>
		</paragraphs>

		<paragraphs>
			<title>Already have a pile of LaTeX?</title>
			<p>
				PreTeXt.Plus includes a LaTeX-to-PreTeXt converter. Treat its output as a
				<em>starting point</em> rather than a finished conversion — some things will
				need cleanup — but for a first look at how your existing material would feel
				in PreTeXt, it is a quick win.
			</p>
		</paragraphs>

		<paragraphs>
			<title>Install PreTeXt locally (long-term)</title>
			<p>
				Eventually you may want PreTeXt running on your own machine. The official
				documentation at <url href="https://pretextbook.org"><c>pretextbook.org</c></url>
				walks through the options. No rush — the Codespaces workflow will serve you
				well for a long time.
			</p>
		</paragraphs>

		<paragraphs>
			<title>Community and help</title>
			<p>
				The PreTeXt community is active and welcoming. Start at
				<url href="https://pretextbook.org/community.html"><c>pretextbook.org/community.html</c></url>
				for forums, documentation, and the author's guide.
			</p>
		</paragraphs>

		<p>
			<em>PreTeXt workshop · your Codespace and your published site will still be there tomorrow.</em>
		</p>
	</section>

</chapter>
